{0 Sockets}

{!Workero.Socket} opens outbound TCP connections, like [connect] from
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/tcp-sockets/}[cloudflare:sockets]}.
Use it to talk to a database, a mail server or any other TCP service that
HTTP does not reach.

There is nothing to configure. [workero build] writes [index.js] with
[import { connect } from "cloudflare:sockets"] on every build, so a program that
does not use sockets pays nothing for it.

{1 Use}

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let socket = Socket.connect ~host:"gopher.floodgap.com" ~port:70 () in
  Socket.opened socket;
  let writer = Socket.writer socket in
  Stream.Writer.write writer "/\r\n";
  Stream.Writer.close writer;
  let reader = Socket.reader socket in
  let buf = Buffer.create 1024 in
  let rec loop () =
    match Stream.Reader.read reader with
    | Some chunk -> Buffer.add_string buf chunk; loop ()
    | None -> ()
  in
  loop ();
  Socket.close socket;
  Response.text (Buffer.contents buf)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Socket.connect} returns at once. A refused connection shows in
  {!Workero.Socket.opened}, which suspends until the connection is up.
- {!Workero.Socket.reader} and {!Workero.Socket.writer} give the
  {!Workero.Stream} readers and writers. Each locks its stream, so call each one
  once for each socket, or unlock the stream with
  {!Workero.Stream.Reader.release} or {!Workero.Stream.Writer.release}. {!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} waits when the server is
  slow to read (back-pressure).
- {!Workero.Stream.Reader.read} gives [None] when the server ends its side.
  {!Workero.Stream.Writer.close} ends yours.
- {!Workero.Socket.close} closes both sides. {!Workero.Socket.closed} waits for
  the socket to close.

{1 TLS}

[~secure_transport:On] uses TLS from the first byte. For a protocol that starts
in plain text, such as PostgreSQL, connect with [~secure_transport:Starttls],
exchange the opening messages, unlock the streams, then call
{!Workero.Socket.start_tls}:

{@ocaml[
let socket = Socket.connect ~secure_transport:Starttls ~host ~port:5432 () in
let reader = Socket.reader socket and writer = Socket.writer socket in
Stream.Reader.release reader;
Stream.Writer.release writer;
let secure = Socket.start_tls socket in
let reader = Socket.reader secure and writer = Socket.writer secure in
]}

[start_tls] returns a new socket. The old socket stops working. The runtime
fails the upgrade while a reader or writer still locks a stream, so release both
first.

{1 Half-open sockets}

By default, when the server ends its side, the runtime also closes your write
side. Pass [~allow_half_open:true] to keep it open, for a protocol where the
client sends after the server has finished.

{1 Test}

[workerd] can run a [connect] handler in a Worker that is the [globalOutbound]
of the Worker under test. That Worker then plays the TCP server. The tests of
workero do this: see [test/worker/upstream.mjs]. With this setup, TLS cannot be
tested: [~secure_transport:On] fails with "Incoming CONNECT with TLS not
supported", and [start_tls] fails with "startTls() is not supported on this
connection". Use [workerd] with a real network service for TLS.

